  "document_overview": {
    "section_headings": [
      "Dense granular materials exhibit complex behaviors upon shear.",
      "In this letter, we apply cyclic shear to a three-dimensional (3D) disordered granular system.",
      "Our granular system consists of bi-disperse smooth plastic beads",
      "We prepare the initial state of the system by applying thousands of cyclic shear cycles which ensures that a steady state is reached",
      "We calculate the global volume fraction based on radical Voronoi tessellation",
      "Empirical constitutive relations are ordinarily employed to account for macroscopic behaviors",
      "Delaunay tessellation partitions the packing structure into non-overlapping tetrahedra",
      "We first show how the topological rearrangement and distortion of N-ring structures leads to the global volume variation of our system.",
      "Since our system has reached steady state by initial preparation, we adopt the granular thermodynamic framework originally developed by Edwards and coworkers",
      "Analogous to the thermal excitation of point defects in crystals, we presume that the number of different N-ring structures are governed by",
      "Other than volume, we also seek the microscopic structural origin of macroscopic shear force response through the evolution of the Delaunay network.",
      "Local topological rearrangements not only induce volume fluctuations, they also relax the stress in the system and therefore should be strongly correlated with macroscopic shear force.",
      "To account for this disparity, we also have to take Delaunay network distortion induced contact level variation into account.",
      "To account for the complex shear force behavior within one cycle, we assume that both particle level topological rearrangement and contact evolution contribute linearly to the shear force to the first approximation.",
      "In summary, by taking friction-induced effect into consideration, combined with the knowledge of particle level topological rearrangements, we can explain the highly transient global volume and the shear force variations of a cyclically sheared granular system.",
      "However, it is obvious that the specific fraction of contributions from these two microscopic mechanisms should be strongly correlated with the microscopic friction coefficient of granular particles which will be system dependent.",
      "The work is supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China",
